vmlab (this lab), 2026-11-13: d3d10umd: a Chromium GPU process gets Microsoft's d3d12.dll

The driver package replaces System32's d3d12.dll with its Direct3D 12 bridge
(vkd3d-proton), which Microsoft has not signed. A Chromium GPU process (Chrome,
Edge, Electron, WebView2, CEF) takes only Microsoft-signed DLLs once it runs.
When a page makes it look for video encoders it delay-loads d3d12.dll, Windows
refuses the bridge (error 577), the process ends itself on purpose, and after
three of those the browser has no WebGL. The lab therefore kept Microsoft's
file in System32, and native Direct3D 12 programs had no device.

Such a process opens this driver's adapter before the restriction starts. The
driver now loads Microsoft's d3d12.dll there, from a copy under the same file
name in System32\vmlab-win3d\native (guest-scripts/d3d12-mode.ps1 makes it).
The loader resolves a later load of the bare name to the module that is
already loaded, in any folder, also with LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_SYSTEM32
(guest-tests/ldtest.cpp). So the bridge can stay in System32 for every other
process.

Only a process whose command line has --type=gpu-process is touched, and only
when the copy exists. D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_NATIVE_D3D12_PRELOAD=0 turns it off.
One line per such process goes to yttrium-user.log.

Not covered: a Chromium GPU process that never opens this adapter (software
rendering) and still loads d3d12.dll.

Applies after guest-patches/9003.

diff --git a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
index e850214..9827452 100644
--- a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Adapter.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,9 @@
 
 #include "util/u_memory.h"
 
+#include <string.h>
+#include <wchar.h>
+
 
 static HRESULT APIENTRY CloseAdapter(D3D10DDI_HADAPTER hAdapter);
 
@@ -58,6 +61,54 @@ ExposeD3D11_1()
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * The driver package puts its Direct3D 12 bridge (vkd3d-proton) in place of
+ * System32's d3d12.dll.  The bridge is not signed by Microsoft, and a
+ * Chromium GPU process (Chrome, Edge, Electron, WebView2, CEF) takes only
+ * Microsoft-signed DLLs once it has started: its later delay-load of
+ * d3d12.dll is refused (error 577) and it ends itself on purpose.
+ *
+ * Such a process opens this driver's adapter first, before that restriction.
+ * So load Microsoft's own d3d12.dll here, from the copy the lab keeps under
+ * the same file name in another folder: the loader resolves a later load of
+ * the bare name "d3d12.dll" to a module of that name that is already loaded,
+ * whatever its folder.  The process then has what stock Windows gives it (no
+ * Direct3D 12 device on this adapter), and every other process the bridge.
+ */
+static void
+PreloadNativeD3D12ForChromiumGpuProcess(void)
+{
+   static LONG done;
+   if (InterlockedExchange(&done, 1))
+      return;
+
+   if (!yttrium_gdi_debug_get_bool_option(
+          "D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_NATIVE_D3D12_PRELOAD", true))
+      return;
+
+   const wchar_t *command_line = GetCommandLineW();
+   if (!command_line || !wcsstr(command_line, L"--type=gpu-process"))
+      return;
+
+   if (GetModuleHandleW(L"d3d12.dll"))
+      return;
+
+   static const wchar_t tail[] = L"\\vmlab-win3d\\native\\d3d12.dll";
+   wchar_t path[MAX_PATH];
+   const UINT len = GetSystemDirectoryW(path, MAX_PATH);
+   if (!len || len + sizeof(tail) / sizeof(tail[0]) > MAX_PATH)
+      return;
+   memcpy(path + len, tail, sizeof(tail));
+
+   if (GetFileAttributesW(path) == INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES)
+      return;
+
+   HMODULE native = LoadLibraryExW(path, NULL, LOAD_WITH_ALTERED_SEARCH_PATH);
+   yttrium_gdi_user_logf("d3d10umd: Chromium GPU process, native d3d12.dll %s error=%lu\n",
+                         native ? "preloaded" : "not loaded",
+                         native ? 0ul : (unsigned long)GetLastError());
+}
+
 /*
  * ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  *
@@ -79,6 +130,8 @@ OpenAdapterCommon(__inout D3D10DDIARG_OPENADAPTER *pOpenData)   // IN
 #endif
    ++numAdapters;
 
+   PreloadNativeD3D12ForChromiumGpuProcess();
+
    Adapter *pAdaptor = (Adapter *)calloc(sizeof *pAdaptor, 1);
